module Core

import Numerics.Integer

# ============================================================================
# Shortest round-trip text of a B128 without big integers
# ============================================================================
# Schubfach (see FloatConvert.rf) needs 10^-k to about twice the significand's width, 256 bits here, over
# k = -4966 .. 4898, too large a table to store whole. So 10^n is built as 10^(64a) * 10^b from two small
# tables (the second exact) within 2^-254, the value and its interval ends are scaled by it into 256-bit
# fixed point with 128 fraction bits (within 2 units), and every decision Schubfach makes (which candidate
# lies inside the interval, which of two is closer) is taken only when the approximation leaves more than
# 8 units of room. Otherwise, which happens when a decision sits exactly on its boundary (an interval end
# that is itself a short decimal, or a tie between two candidates), the exact big-integer method decides.

# 10^(64a) for a = -97 .. 96 (the whole D128 range), as 256-bit significands in [2^255, 2^256) rounded to nearest (hi, lo),
# the exponent being floor(log2(10^(64a))) - 255.
